                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Not inherently bad, the story mode feels like you asked chat GPT to write some post-apocalyptic tale and the dialogue doesn't help, but that could also be in part translation issues if the devs aren't native English speakers.\n\nHard/Normal changes are very minimal in the story progression, adding mostly new blockers you need to think on your feet to crack open (e.g., when you get springs for the first time you cannot go directly to the back wall without shooting a ball at the phased blocker in hard mode). Endings are also collectibles based, and you are likely to miss your ride for one ending as collectibles appear before notes about them are made. They are however easy to find as they seem to only be possible to pick up in the observation area with one or two exceptions. \n\nMy primary complaint of the game is the physics, namely that it is wildly inconsistent. You can place a spring trap under the dummy and reload multiple times trying to get the same result twice - same goes for mines with much clearer problems as the ragdoll may never touch a mine in some loads. This is made worse by the game not loading quickly when accessing the level from the elevator, or when starting/stopping the simulation. If one of those two problems were amended, the other would be less of a pain in the ass without question as you would have to do less reloads over a bad start or your reloads would be quicker at least if it were optimized more cleanly, especially when looking at pre-placed assets in a level. \n\nAs a saving grace, the puzzles of story mode allow you to tackle objectives across multiple simulations. You can solve for one objective, then sell everything back and solve for the next.",
